--TEST--
`Core\Html::escape` carries text holding none of the five characters through unchanged, and escapes an input that already reads as a character reference rather than leaving it alone — `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named`, `rule:errors/ambiguous-input-refused`, `rule:core-classes/html-escape-answers-markup`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The other side of the boundary the sibling case pins. A launderer that
// escaped something it did not have to would corrupt every document it
// touched, and this asks the question by *comparison* rather than by printing
// the answer: a member that rewrote one extra code point fails here while its
// own line still looks right. The comparison goes through `toSource`, because
// `rule:core-classes/html-escape-answers-markup` gives the answer a type `==` has no row against a `string` for.
string $plain = "ordinary text, punctuation included: a-b_c/d.e (f) [g] {h} 100% ~ | ^";
echo Core\Html::toSource(Core\Html::escape($plain), "comparing the escape against its input") == $plain
     ? "same" : "changed", "\n";

// Not ASCII either. `string` is UTF-8 by `rule:types/bytes`, so there is no `$encoding`
// argument and nothing here to transliterate.
string $text = "日本語 — naïve café 👍";
echo Core\Html::toSource(Core\Html::escape($text), "comparing the escape against its input") == $text
     ? "same" : "changed", "\n";

// ADR 0095: ambiguous input is refused, never repaired. PHP's fourth
// `htmlspecialchars` argument exists to suppress this — `$double_encode` — and
// there is no spelling for it here, because `&amp;` in the input is text that
// said `&amp;` and guessing otherwise means a document that meant to display
// an escape silently loses it. Escaping is not idempotent and is not meant to
// be.
echo Core\Html::escape("&amp;"), "\n";

// And that non-idempotence is exactly `rule:security/launderer-answers-a-carrier`'s second condition, so the
// nested spelling of the line above no longer compiles: `escape` takes a
// `tainted string` and its own answer is a `Markup`. Saying it twice is now a
// deliberate act that has to name a reason, and it produces what it always
// produced.
echo Core\Html::escape(Core\Html::toSource(Core\Html::escape("&"), "escaping the escape on purpose")), "\n";
--EXPECT--
same
same
&amp;amp;
&amp;amp;
